  "polyhedral_union_ray_transport_lemma": {
    "causal_type_consequence": "The retained rays are reflected copies or fixed glue rays, so their finite/ideal/hyperideal type is unchanged; flattening only removes rays.",
    "proof": "Away from h the union is locally one parent copy. On h any candidate is an extreme ray of the parent glue face, hence a parent extreme ray. Deleting h lowers active-normal rank by at most one. If all remaining active normals are h-orthogonal, their reflected span is unchanged and has deficient rank. If some active a is nonorthogonal, s_h(a)-a is a nonzero multiple of h, so the doubled active normals restore full rank.",
    "statement": "For a convex Coxeter double C union s_h(C), the complete extreme-ray list consists of two reflected copies of each parent ray off h, plus each parent ray on h whose active set contains a wall not orthogonal to h; an on-h ray with all other active walls orthogonal to h is flattened. No other extreme rays occur."
  },
